comments:
Thanks for filing this!

I believe that the restriction of rejecting VirtualHosts with no usable routes dates all the way back to our implementation of [gRFC A28](https://github.com/grpc/proposal/blob/master/A28-xds-traffic-splitting-and-routing.md#response-validation).  (It doesn't look like this particular restriction was explicitly spelled out in the gRFC, but I believe we implemented this restriction when we implemented xDS routing.)  However, we've already essentially eliminated the benefit of that restriction by the changes made in [gRFC A36](https://github.com/grpc/proposal/blob/master/A36-xds-for-servers.md), where we introduced a new route action type for servers.  Since the XdsClient does not know whether a given RDS resource will be used on client side or server side, it needs to allow both types of route action in the resource validation code, and then the L7 code has to deal with the case where an RPC hits a route whose action type is invalid in that context (e.g., a `NonForwardingAction` route on the client, or a `RouteAction` route on the server).  Since we're doing that, it means that the L7 code also needs to deal with the case where an RPC does not actually match any of the routes, which is essentially what would happen if there were no routes in the list.  Given that, it probably would be consistent to just allow empty route lists.

@ejona86 @dfawley Do Java and Go have a similar check currently?  Any objections to eliminating this check?
